/* book Worked example 2: Q: Take x[n] = [1, 2, 3, 4] and h[n] = [1, 1, 0, 0]. (a) Find the 4-point circular convolution y[n] directly. (b) Check it with the DFTs, using X[k] from Worked example 1. (c) Find the linear convolution and say how long the DFT must be to give it.
   1. Circular sum: y[n] = Σm=03 x[m] h[(n−m) mod 4]. Since h has only h[0] = h[1] = 1, each output is y[n] = x[n] + x[(n−1) mod 4].
   2. y[0] = x[0] + x[3] = 1 + 4 = 5 (the index −1 wraps to 3). y[1] = x[1] + x[0] = 2 + 1 = 3. y[2] = 3 + 2 = 5. y[3] = 4 + 3 = 7. So y = [5, 3, 5, 7].
   3. DFT of h: H[k] = 1 + W4k, so H = [2, 1 − j, 0, 1 + j]. From Worked example 1, X = [10, −2 + 2j, −2, −2 − 2j].
   4. Multiply bin by bin: Y[0] = 10 × 2 = 20; Y[1] = (−2 + 2j)(1 − j) = −2 + 2j + 2j − 2j² = 4j; Y[2] = (−2)(0) = 0; Y[3] = (−2 − 2j)(1 + j) = −2 − 2j − 2j − 2j² = −4j.
   5. Inverse DFT with W4−nk = jnk: y[0] = (20 + 4j + 0 − 4j)/4 = 5; y[1] = (20 + 4j·j + 0 + (−4j)(−j))/4 = (20 − 4 − 4)/4 = 3. Both match the direct sum.
   6. Linear convolution: ylin = [1, 3, 5, 7, 4], length 4 + 2 − 1 = 5. The circular result is this with the last value 4 wrapped onto n = 0: 1 + 4 = 5.
   7. To get the linear result from DFTs, zero-pad both sequences to L ≥ 5 points before transforming.
   Answer: Circular: y = [5, 3, 5, 7]; the DFT product gives the same values. Linear: [1, 3, 5, 7, 4], which needs a DFT of length 5 or more. */
