/* book: lead: The z-transform turns a sequence x[n] into a function X(z) of a complex variable z. It exists because the DTFT does not converge for every sequence. Weighting each sample by r−n, with r = |z|, lets the sum converge in a ring of the z-plane. That ring, the region of convergence or ROC, is part of the answer. Without it, the algebra cannot tell a right-sided sequence from a left-sided one. Engineers */
