/* book Worked example: Q: Find X(z), the poles, the zeros and the ROC for x[n] = (1/3)nu[n] − (1/2)nu[−n − 1]. State whether the DTFT exists.
   1. First term: set a = 1/3. The pair anu[n] ↔ z/(z − a) gives X1(z) = z/(z − 1/3), ROC |z| > 1/3.
   2. Second term: set a = 1/2. The pair −anu[−n − 1] ↔ z/(z − a) with ROC |z| < |a| gives X2(z) = z/(z − 1/2), ROC |z| < 1/2.
   3. Add: X(z) = z/(z − 1/3) + z/(z − 1/2).
   4. Common denominator: X(z) = z[(z − 1/2) + (z − 1/3)] / [(z − 1/3)(z − 1/2)] = z(2z − 5/6) / [(z − 1/3)(z − 1/2)].
   5. Poles solve z − 1/3 = 0 and z − 1/2 = 0: z = 1/3 and z = 1/2. Zeros solve z = 0 and 2z − 5/6 = 0: z = 0 and z = 5/12 ≈ 0.4167.
   6. ROC is the overlap |z| > 1/3 and |z| < 1/2, so 1/3 < |z| < 1/2.
   7. The unit circle has |z| = 1. It is not in the ROC because 1 > 1/2. Therefore the DTFT does not exist.
   Answer: X(z) = z(2z − 5/6)/[(z − 1/3)(z − 1/2)], poles at z = 1/3 and z = 1/2, zeros at z = 0 and z = 5/12, ROC 1/3 < |z| < 1/2; no DTFT exists. */
