/* book Worked example 2: Q: A finite sequence has x[−1] = 1, x[0] = 2, x[1] = 3 and is zero elsewhere. Find X(z), its ROC, and X(z) at z = 1 and z = −1.
   1. Put each sample into X(z) = Σ x[n] z−n: X(z) = 1·z1 + 2·z0 + 3·z−1 = z + 2 + 3z−1.
   2. The term z blows up as |z| → ∞, so z = ∞ is excluded (the sample at n = −1 makes it so).
   3. The term 3z−1 blows up at z = 0, so z = 0 is excluded (the sample at n = 1 makes it so).
   4. Every other z gives a finite sum of three terms, so the ROC is 0 < |z| < ∞.
   5. X(1) = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6, the plain sum of the samples. X(−1) = −1 + 2 − 3 = −2, the alternating sum.
   6. Both points lie on the unit circle, which is inside the ROC, so they are the DTFT at ω = 0 and ω = π.
   Answer: X(z) = z + 2 + 3z−1, ROC 0 < |z| < ∞ (z = 0 and z = ∞ excluded); X(1) = 6 and X(−1) = −2. */
